description:
`ElixirASTPrinter` documents itself as formatting-only but still injects exception declarations, scans and qualifies Repo aliases/modules, allocates loop names through static state, and performs other target-policy rewrites. Move reviewed semantic policies to builders or named passes one family at a time so printing becomes deterministic rendering of printer-legal structural AST.

## FILES

- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/ElixirASTPrinter.hx`
- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/ElixirAST.hx` transformation helpers that do not belong in definitions
- module/dependency/exception/loop builders and focused transforms
- request-local `PrintContext` and naming plan
- printer legality validator
- focused snapshots/runtime tests and handwritten-output corpus
- architecture and generated-output documentation

## ALGORITHM

1. Inventory each printer branch that selects semantics, injects declarations/imports/aliases, allocates correctness-visible names, deletes nodes, or infers application/module policy.
2. Move one family per reviewed commit to the earliest truthful owner: exception declarations to module construction/legalization; Repo dependencies and qualification to typed dependency/alias planning; loop names to request-local allocation; remaining normalizations to named AST passes.
3. Keep precedence, parentheses, indentation, escaping, comments, and source-map placement in the printer.
4. Introduce immutable `PrintContext` and prove rendering the same AST/context yields the same bytes under cold, warm, reordered, and repeated runs.
5. Keep unresolved semantic/raw markers printer-illegal and report the owner/phase that failed; never add a printer fallback.
6. Move `applyIdiomaticEnumTransformation` out of the AST definition module only after its pass ownership and replay behavior are proven.

## FAILURE MODES

- moving several unrelated policies with a broad expected-output refresh;
- replacing printer policy with a late name/path/text heuristic pass;
- changing Repo/exception/module ABI or public output accidentally;
- allocating names after semantic passes can no longer observe collisions;
- source-map drift hidden by formatting equality;
- teaching the printer to lower receiver or managed-reference markers;
- deleting replay/final passes because their names look redundant.

## TESTS

- byte parity for each migrated policy family before intentional quality changes;
- exception, Repo, module qualification, loop-name collision, enum, and source-map fixtures;
- repeat/warm/order determinism;
- printer legality negative fixtures;
- handwritten target-shape review and warnings-as-errors;
- runtime and package parity for affected framework/core paths.

## VERIFICATION

Run focused printer/policy tests, all affected snapshot chunks, result and semantic invariants, handwritten-output, pass docs/inventory/scope guards, examples/output/WAE/runtime, source maps, source/package parity, async todo-app sentinel plus Playwright, and exact-head CI/CodeQL.

## ROLLOUT

Incremental internal cleanup. Land one policy owner at a time, preserve bytes by default, and keep deliberate idiomatic improvements separate. No profile, printer repair mode, or target-text postprocessor is introduced.

`FunctionResultInvariant` currently models numeric tails that the printer later deletes, so a semantic verifier depends on a hidden rewrite in the formatting layer. Move numeric-sentinel ownership into the AST pipeline and make normal/abrupt completion independent of printer behavior.

## FILES

- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/validation/FunctionResultInvariant.hx`
- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/ElixirASTPrinter.hx`
- the earliest builder or one named cleanup pass that currently produces/removes numeric sentinels
- pass registry/phase documentation
- focused positive/negative result fixtures
- handwritten-output corpus and affected generated snapshots

## ALGORITHM

1. Inventory every printer rule that drops or rewrites numeric sentinels and trace each producer to its earliest typed/structural owner.
2. Eliminate the sentinel at construction where safe or move deletion to one result-position-aware AST cleanup pass.
3. Model normal completion, value production, unit/statement completion, abrupt completion, and explicit opaque-raw authority in the result lattice.
4. Preserve the existing authored non-`Void` contracts and first-degrading-pass attribution through transparent granular execution.
5. Give synthesized definitions an explicit Value, Void, or Unchecked contract where their result matters; do not infer validity from printer behavior.
6. Remove printer-specific numeric-tail exceptions only after byte/runtime parity proves the AST owner is complete.

## FAILURE MODES

- deleting a real authored numeric result;
- treating raise/throw or guaranteed transfer as a missing value;
- assuming every nonempty raw string is a valid value without authority;
- weakening the invariant or source return type to preserve a historical printer workaround;
- moving all printer policy in one unreviewable change;
- accepting snapshot parity without runtime proof for zero/one results and zero-iteration paths.

## TESTS

- authored Int/Float zero and one tails in blocks, `do`, branches, loops, closures, and exception paths;
- zero-iteration and abrupt-completion joins;
- synthesized Value/Void/Unchecked contracts;
- raw expression versus statement authority;
- first-degrading granular pass diagnostic;
- old/new generated byte and BEAM runtime parity;
- no remaining result-verifier reference to printer deletion.

## VERIFICATION

Run `npm run test:result-invariant`, focused Haxe-authored runtime tests, affected/full snapshots, handwritten-output, pass docs/inventory/scope guards, examples/output/WAE/runtime, source/package parity, async todo-app sentinel plus Playwright, and exact-head CI/CodeQL.

## ROLLOUT

Migrate one sentinel producer family at a time. Require byte parity first; isolate any real correctness fix with an ordinary-Haxe regression and separately reviewed generated diff. The printer retains fail-fast legality but no result repair.

Passes and the printer currently use process-global counters/context, while cached or reused analyses have no general preservation/invalidation contract. Add request-local pass infrastructure and conservative analysis ownership without forcing all legacy passes to claim false precision at once.

## FILES

- `src/reflaxe/elixir/CompilationContext.hx`
- new pass/compile context, temp allocator, pass outcome, and analysis manager types under `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/**`
- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/ElixirASTTransformer.hx`
- `src/reflaxe/elixir/ast/PassApplicability.hx`
- pass registry/introspection types
- pass metrics and structural digest helpers
- deterministic and forced-recomputation tests
- compiler architecture and pass documentation

## ALGORITHM

1. Add request-local `PassContext` ownership for stable pass identity, phase/scope, diagnostics, AST revision, analysis cache, and collision-aware temporary allocation.
2. Introduce a pass outcome contract that can represent legacy uncertainty honestly. A legacy adapter may conservatively report changed/unknown; it must not claim unchanged merely because root identity or printed bytes happen to match.
3. Add a lazy request-local `AnalysisManager` keyed by AST revision with transitive dependency invalidation. Changed or unknown passes invalidate all cached analyses unless preservation is explicitly proven.
4. Keep current `PassApplicability` snapshot behavior until its dependency and preservation contracts are audited. Do not enable a 578-times-per-module recomputation regression merely to claim conservative invalidation.
5. Add forced-recomputation validation that compares cached and fresh results before migrating a correctness-sensitive analysis such as module capabilities, variable use, result flow, raw authority, or semantic-family counts.
6. Replace printer-based debug change detection with an explicit outcome or structural digest that can inspect printer-illegal intermediate AST.
7. Migrate static counters/current-module state only in the task that owns the corresponding semantic behavior; this task supplies the context and allocator rather than combining receiver/printer rewrites.

## FAILURE MODES

- a Boolean `changed=false` inferred from object identity when a pass mutates nested state;
- stale capability/use/result analysis after a rewrite;
- invalidating and recomputing every expensive analysis after all 578 legacy passes without a budget;
- retaining process-global name allocation or application context in newly migrated owners;
- using printed Elixir as the correctness cache key or change detector;
- requiring all legacy passes to declare complete read/write sets in one rewrite;
- analysis behavior changing generated output in the scaffolding slice.

## TESTS

- request isolation across repeated, warm, reordered, and parallel compilation;
- temp-name collision and deterministic allocation;
- changed/unchanged/legacy-unknown invalidation behavior;
- transitive analysis dependency invalidation;
- cached-versus-forced-recomputed differential checks;
- printer-illegal marker change tracking;
- no-output-drift and bounded compile-time/memory baselines.

## VERIFICATION

Run focused context/analysis tests, warm-server determinism, pass docs/inventory/scope guards, bounded profiles, all affected snapshots, result invariants, examples, package parity, handwritten-output, async todo-app sentinel plus Playwright, and exact-head CI/CodeQL.

## ROLLOUT

Land request-local scaffolding output-inert. Migrate one analysis and one state owner at a time only after forced-recompute parity. Legacy uncertainty remains conservative and visible; it is not a reason for a permanent alternate pipeline.

Normal compilation currently exposes seven executable bundle passes whose bodies invoke 578 granular passes through a second hidden runner. Inner passes are therefore invisible to the outer runner's result attribution, timing, snapshots, future legality checks, and analysis invalidation. Replace opaque executable bundles with transparent nested pipeline groups while preserving their exact behavior.

## FILES

